(t+1) = exp(-y * (D - 1))  [if D is time to maturity]\n\n# Wait - if we're observing the same position, the duration changes \n# as time passes. But the problem might assume duration stays constant?\n\n# Let me re-read: \"Macaulay style duration that makes the two observed \n# values consistent with a constant yield\"\n\n# If duration is constant (not time to maturity), then:\n# P(t) = exp(-y * D)\n# P(t+1) = exp(-y * D)  [same duration, same yield]\n# But that would give same price, which is wrong.\n\n# Let's think of it differently - perhaps these are prices of bonds \n# with different times to maturity, but we observe them at consecutive periods?\n\n# Actually the most sensible interpretation: we observe the price of a \n# zero-coupon bond position at time t and t+1. The duration changes as \n# time passes (D becomes D-1 after one period). We find D that is \n# consistent with constant yield y.\n\n# At time t: P(t) = exp(-y * D)\n# At time t+1: P(t+1) = exp(-y * (D - 1))\n\n# Taking ratio: P(t+1)/P(t) = exp(y)\n# So: exp(y) = 6207/4187\n# y = ln(6207/4187)\n\n# Then from P(t) = exp(-y * D):\n# D = -ln(P(t))/y\n\ny = math.log(P_t1 / P_t)\nprint(f\"Alternative yield (if price increases): y = {y}\")\n\nD = -math.log(P_t) / y\nprint(f\"Macaulay duration D = {D}\")\n\n# Wait, but I need to check which interpretation is correct\n# If P(t+1)/P(t) = exp(y), then:\n# y = ln(6207/4187) = ln(1.4823...) ≈ 0.393\n\n# Then D = -ln(4187)/0.393 ≈ ?\n\nprint(f\"\\nVerification:\")\nprint(f\"P(t) = exp(-y*D) = {math.exp(-y*D)}\")\nprint(f\"P(t+1) = exp(-y*(D-1)) = {math.exp(-y*(D-1))}\")\n\n# Round to 4 decimal places (unrounded value first)\nprint(f\"\\nFinal D (unrounded): {D}\")\nprint(f\"Final D (4 decimals): {D:.4f}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
