{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775333404-71cvfVoJAW6zXPT5sTwb","role":"assistant","created":1775333421,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, the problem says \"Macaulay style duration\" - Macaulay duration is typically positive. But in my derivation, I'm getting negative values. \n\nLet me reconsider the problem. The key insight might be that we should think about this differently:\n\n- We observe prices at two points, one year apart\n- These are prices of a \"zero coupon position\" \n- We want to find the Macaulay duration that makes them consistent with constant yield\n\nLet me think about the fundamental equation. For a zero-coupon bond:\nP = e^(-yT) where T is time to maturity\n\nAt time t (FY 1953): P₁ = e^(-yD) where D is duration (time to maturity)\nAt time t+1 (FY 1954): P₂ = e^(-y(D-1)) \n\nThe ratio: P₂/P₁ = e^(-y(D-1)) / e^(-yD) = e^y\n\nSo: e^y = 6207/4187\ny = ln(6207/4187) = 0.3937\n\nThen from P₁ = e^(-yD):\nD = -ln(P₁)/y = -ln(4187)/0.3937 = -21.18\n\nThis gives negative duration mathematically.\n\nBut wait - maybe I have the ratio backwards. What if it's:\nP₂/P₁ = e^(-y)?\n\nThen y = -ln(6207/4187) = -0.3937 (negative yield)\n\nThen D = -ln(4187)/(-0.3937) = 21.18 (positive)\n\nLet me verify this model properly:\n\nIf P(t+1) = P(t) * e^(-y), then for y = -0.3937:\nP(1954) = P(1953) * e^(-(-0.3937)) = 4187 * e^(0.3937) = 4187 * 1.4824 ≈ 6207 ✓\n\n\nThis seems to work! But wait - I'm using a different formula now, one that assumes the same duration at both times.\n\nActually no - the standard formula for zero coupon bond price with continuous compounding is:\nP = e^(-yT) where T is time to maturity\n\nIf we observe at two times, we have different T values:\nAt t: T = D (let's say)\nAt t+1: T = D-1\n\nBut the yield is CONSTANT. That's the key constraint.\n\nSo we have:\nP(t) = e^(-yD)\nP(t+1) = e^(-y(D-1))\n\nTaking the ratio:\nP(t+1)/P(t) = e^(-y(D-1))/e^(-yD) = e^y\n\nThis is correct. The only way to make this work with positive yi